# Text of the literature review. Markup: *italic*. Every claim about a source is limited to
# what was read in its abstract or full text (see research/lit_review_relevance.md).
TITLE = 'Geospatial-Based Flood Risk Modelling and Predictive Analysis Using Artificial Intelligence: A Review for Malé, Maldives'
AUTHOR = '[Name] & [student no.]'

C = []
def H(t): C.append(('H', t))
def S(t): C.append(('S', t))
def P(t): C.append(('P', t))

H('ABSTRACT')
P('Machine learning is now widely used to map where floods are likely to occur, but most published methods assume data that small, densely built atoll cities do not have. This review asks what the literature offers for one narrow question: which areas of Malé island proper, Maldives, are prone to rainfall-induced flooding? Thirty-two journal articles are reviewed under four themes: data-driven flood susceptibility methods, learning from positive-only evidence, validation of spatial models, and data limits on low-lying islands. Urban flood studies report high accuracy, but they usually rely on hundreds or thousands of flood points, drainage data and random train–test splits. Work on presence-only data in ecology offers better-founded tools for biased, positive-only records. Spatially separated validation can show that the predictive skill suggested by random validation is largely illusory. No peer-reviewed study of rain flooding in Malé was found. The review ends by proposing a cautious, descriptive-first approach, evaluated with spatial blocks and presence-only metrics.')

H('1 INTRODUCTION')
P('Machine learning (ML) has become a common tool in flood research because it can learn complex relationships from data at a much lower computational cost than physically based models [Mosavi, Ozturk and Chau, 2018; Bentivoglio *et al.*, 2022]. One of its most frequent uses is flood susceptibility mapping: ranking places by how likely they are to flood, given local conditions such as elevation, land cover and drainage.')
P('The Maldives is among the lowest-lying countries in the world, with land about 1 m above mean sea level [Brown *et al.*, 2020]. Published research on flooding in atoll nations has concentrated on sea-level rise and wave-driven overwash [Storlazzi *et al.*, 2018; Brown *et al.*, 2020]. Malé, the densely built capital island, also floods after heavy rain, when water collects in streets faster than it can drain. Local news and agency reports describe this repeatedly, but it has received little scientific attention.')
P('This review supports a project that asks: *which areas of Malé island proper are prone to rainfall-induced flooding?* The aim is area susceptibility mapping, not forecasting when the next flood will happen. The study area is Malé island proper, excluding Hulhumalé and Vilimalé. Coastal and wave flooding is treated as a separate process.')
P('The literature was searched using combinations of the terms "flood susceptibility", "urban pluvial", "machine learning", "presence-only", "positive-unlabelled", "non-flood sampling", "spatial cross-validation", "digital elevation model accuracy", "Maldives" and "atoll". Searches ran in web search engines and the Crossref database, and the reference lists of key papers were followed up. Only peer-reviewed journal articles in English were considered. Thirty-nine candidates were screened by title and abstract, and thirty-two were retained because they addressed at least one of the four themes. Several retained articles come from ecology, where the problems of biased presence-only records and spatially structured validation have been studied in most depth.')
P('Section 2 defines the key terms and summarises flooding on atolls. Sections 3 to 6 review the four themes. Section 7 identifies research gaps and the approach they suggest for Malé, and Section 8 concludes.')

H('2 BACKGROUND')
S('2.1 Susceptibility, hazard and risk')
P('These terms are often used loosely, but they describe different targets. *Susceptibility* is the relative likelihood that a place floods, given its physical setting. It says nothing about the depth, timing or frequency of flooding. *Hazard* adds magnitude or probability, for example the area flooded in a 1% annual event. Mobley *et al.* (2021) show that binary regulatory hazard zones hide much of the variation in property risk. *Risk* combines hazard with the exposure and vulnerability of people and assets: Darabi *et al.* (2019), for example, combine a hazard map with a vulnerability index built from building quality, population density and socio-economic factors. This review concerns susceptibility only. A susceptibility map built from the evidence available for Malé can rank areas, but it cannot give the probability that a street floods in a given storm.')
S('2.2 Flooding on atolls and in the Maldives')
P('Storlazzi *et al.* (2018) project that, at current emission rates, interactions between sea-level rise and waves over reefs will cause annual wave-driven overwash of most atoll islands by the mid-21st century. Frequent damage to infrastructure, and freshwater aquifers that could not recover between overwash events, would make many islands uninhabitable. For the Maldives, Wadey *et al.* (2017) analysed sea-level records (1987–2015), hindcast waves (1979–2015) and two major flood events, in April 1987 and May 2007. They identified long-period Southern Ocean swell combined with spring tides as a key cause, with wave set-up an essential but poorly quantified mechanism. Brown *et al.* (2020) model extreme water levels on Hulhumalé, an artificial island raised to 1.8 m. They find that overtopping becomes likely at 0.6 ± 0.2 m of sea-level rise, and that land raising has reduced flood risk there. All three studies concern coastal flooding. Rain flooding in a dense, paved city follows a different mechanism: Bersabe and Jun (2025) describe pluvial flooding as occurring when underground drainage is overwhelmed. That process depends on local ground levels, surface sealing and drainage capacity rather than on wave run-up. The searches for this review found no peer-reviewed study of rain flooding in Malé, and that gap motivates the review.')

H('3 DATA-DRIVEN FLOOD SUSCEPTIBILITY METHODS')
S('3.1 Statistical and early machine-learning methods')
P('Early susceptibility studies overlaid a flood inventory on mapped conditioning factors and estimated how strongly each factor class was associated with flooding. Rahmati, Pourghasemi and Zeinivand (2016) used frequency ratio and weights-of-evidence in Golestan Province, Iran. They mapped 144 flood locations from agency records and field surveys and split them randomly into 70% for training and 30% for validation. Their nine factors included lithology, distance from rivers, slope, topographic wetness index and altitude, and the two models reached areas under the ROC curve (AUC) of 76.5% and 74.7%. Tehrany, Pradhan and Jebur (2014) combined weights-of-evidence with a support vector machine (SVM) in an ensemble. These designs suit river catchments, where flooding follows terrain and drainage networks. On a flat, fully paved island with no rivers, most of these factors vary little or are undefined.')
S('3.2 Urban and pluvial studies')
P('Urban studies replace river-based factors with variables describing surfaces and drainage (Table 1). Darabi *et al.* (2019) mapped 113 flooded points in Sari, Iran, from reports and field surveys. With a random 70/30 split, a genetic-algorithm rule model (GARP) outperformed a decision tree (QUEST), with AUC = 93.5% against 89.2%. Tang *et al.* (2019) assessed waterlogging in Guangzhou using an SVM tuned by particle swarm optimisation. They argued that choosing negative (non-flood) samples subjectively, or by a single random draw, produces poor classifiers, and so used repeated random sampling with verification. Seleem *et al.* (2022) drew on 4,333 reported flood locations in Berlin, from fire-brigade, social-media and customer reports. They modelled a 170 km² subset of 1,967 flooded and 1,967 non-flooded locations, split randomly, to compare a convolutional neural network (CNN), an artificial neural network, a random forest (RF) and an SVM at resolutions from 30 m to 2 m. All models reached AUC ≥ 0.87 on test data, and RF performed best. However, most models performed only moderately when predicting outside the training area. Mobley *et al.* (2021) trained an RF on national flood-insurance claims dating back to 1976 across 30,523 km² of south-east Texas. They paired claims, at roughly one to one, with pseudo-absences drawn at random from building footprints, and reported AUC = 0.895 and a test accuracy of 82.2%. In Seoul, Bersabe and Jun (2025) found that adding sewer-pipe density and distance to a storm drain raised RF accuracy by 7.58% and AUC by 3.80%.')
C.append(('TABLE', None))
P('Three patterns stand out. First, these studies use hundreds to thousands of flood locations, far more than are available for Malé. Second, their non-flood samples are constructed, not observed. Zhang *et al.* (2025) kept the same flood points and changed only how non-flood points were chosen: restricting them to low-susceptibility areas and sampling with a one-class SVM. This raised the accuracy of an ensemble model from 0.71 to 0.95 (24 percentage points), compared with random buffer sampling. Reported accuracy therefore reflects the sampling design as well as the model. Third, drainage variables matter in cities [Bersabe and Jun, 2025], yet no open drainage dataset exists for Malé. High AUC values in Table 1 cannot be compared across studies, because each comes from different data, extents and validation designs.')
P('The studies also differ in what counts as a flood. Rahmati, Pourghasemi and Zeinivand (2016) and Darabi *et al.* (2019) combined agency records or reports with field surveys. Mobley *et al.* (2021) used insurance claims, which record losses at insured structures rather than water on the ground. Seleem *et al.* (2022) used fire-brigade, social-media and customer reports, which makes theirs the closest to Malé\'s evidence. Bersabe and Jun (2025) used a record of known flooding points. Each source carries its own bias: claims follow insurance uptake, and reports follow where people look and what they consider newsworthy. The Malé evidence, compiled from news articles and agency statements, resembles the report-based inventories but has far fewer independent locations. For Malé, handling bias (Section 4) therefore matters more than the choice of classifier.')
S('3.3 Deep learning')
P('Bentivoglio *et al.* (2022) reviewed 58 deep-learning studies of flood mapping. Models with convolutional layers were usually more accurate, but "a major challenge lies in developing deep learning models that can generalize to unseen case studies", and the models reviewed gave deterministic outputs with little treatment of uncertainty. Wang, Lyu and Zhang (2023) addressed data scarcity in Dalian with a semi-supervised graph attention network trained on fewer than 1.2% of spatial units, using four basic factors. Even this approach needs a labelled set of flooded and non-flooded units, though a small one. With few independent flood records and no observed dry locations, deep models are unlikely to be justified for Malé.')

H('4 LEARNING FROM POSITIVE-ONLY EVIDENCE')
S('4.1 Presence-only and positive-unlabelled learning')
P('Flood inventories record where flooding was reported, not where it was observed to be absent. This is the same structure as species occurrence records, which is why methods from species distribution modelling transfer to the flood problem. Li *et al.* (2022) note that "non-flood" samples drawn from places without flood records may contain floods that were never recorded, a problem known as case-control sampling with contaminated controls. They apply a positive-unlabelled (PU) algorithm that trains a classifier from flood locations and unlabelled background without needing true negatives. In ecology, Elith *et al.* (2011) explain MaxEnt, which has been used extensively for presence-only modelling. They show that it compares the distribution of covariates at presence sites with their distribution across the landscape. They emphasise two problems: sample bias, and the lack of information on prevalence, meaning how common the outcome is overall. Without prevalence information, absolute probabilities cannot be identified from presence-only data, although places can still be ranked. That limit matches the definition of susceptibility in Section 2.1.')
S('4.2 Reporting bias and record scope')
P('Presence records are rarely collected evenly. Phillips *et al.* (2009) showed that occurrence data are biased towards easily accessed areas, and that drawing background data with the same bias, from a "target group" of records collected the same way, improved average model performance across 226 species. Flood reports in Malé have an obvious analogue: news coverage and pumping operations concentrate on main roads and known problem sites, so the absence of reports elsewhere is not evidence of dryness. Songchon, Wright and Beevers (2021) argue that crowdsourced flood reports become usable alongside conventional observations once their quality, and hence their uncertainty, is assessed. Record geometry matters too. In a discussion article, Al-Abadi and Pradhan (2020) argue that a flood is an areal phenomenon that cannot be represented by a point except at very small map scales (around 1:10,000,000). Taken together, these studies support keeping each Malé record at its reported scope (street, section, landmark or ward) and aggregating it to areal units, rather than treating every report as a precise point.')
S('4.3 Sample size')
P('Wisz *et al.* (2008) evaluated 12 algorithms for 46 species at sample sizes of 100, 30 and 10 records. Accuracy fell and variability rose as samples shrank. Flexible methods that model interactions performed well with large samples but not with the smallest ones, while MaxEnt was among the least sensitive to sample size. No algorithm predicted consistently well with fewer than 30 records. van Proosdij *et al.* (2016) found that the number of records needed for an accurate model depends on prevalence: in a real African study area it ranged from 14 for narrow-ranged to 25 for widespread species, and that AUC computed from presence-only data is itself prevalence-dependent. For Malé, where repeated reports cluster at a small number of locations (only about 11 of the 75 cells in this project\'s 200 m grid contain a located report), these findings point to simple, strongly regularised models with few covariates, and to treating any model output as exploratory.')

H('5 VALIDATION OF SPATIAL MODELS')
S('5.1 Spatial autocorrelation and block cross-validation')
P('Nearby places tend to resemble each other, so a random train–test split leaks information between the two sets. Roberts *et al.* (2017) show that ignoring such structure leads to serious underestimation of predictive error. They warn that structured data also make it easy to overfit with predictors that have no causal link to the outcome. They recommend block cross-validation, while noting that blocking can force extrapolation and so exaggerate errors. Ploton *et al.* (2020) give a striking demonstration: using 11.8 million trees in central Africa, a non-spatial validation suggested that their model explained more than half of the variation in forest biomass (R² = 0.53), while spatial validation revealed quasi-null predictive power (R² of about 0.14). Hijmans (2012) identified "spatial sorting bias" in presence-background evaluation and showed that AUC values were strongly inflated. A null model based only on distance to training sites outperformed MaxEnt for 45% of species. Yoo and Koo (2025) add balance: random cross-validation is optimistic when autocorrelation is moderate or strong, though not when it is weak. Spatial cross-validation reduces that bias, but its settings must match the level of autocorrelation to avoid excessively pessimistic estimates. Tools such as blockCV make spatial, buffered and environmental blocking practical [Valavi *et al.*, 2019].')
P('Few of the flood studies in Section 3 validate this way. Rahmati, Pourghasemi and Zeinivand (2016), Darabi *et al.* (2019) and Seleem *et al.* (2022) split their points randomly. Seleem *et al.* (2022) also tested their models outside the training area, where most performed only moderately. The high accuracies in Table 1 therefore show how well models interpolate within a study area, not how well they transfer to unseen streets.')
S('5.2 Area of applicability')
P('Meyer and Pebesma (2021) argue that a spatial model can be trusted only where the new data resemble the training data. They define an "area of applicability" from a dissimilarity index in predictor space, weighted by variable importance and thresholded using cross-validation. This bears directly on transferring models trained in large mainland cities to a small coral island, whose combination of elevation, density and coastal setting may lie outside any training range.')
S('5.3 Metrics without true absences')
P('AUC is the default metric in the flood studies reviewed, but its use with presence-background data is contested. Lobo, Jiménez-Valverde and Real (2008) give five reasons not to rely on it. The most important is that the extent of the study area strongly affects the rate of correctly predicted absences, and so the AUC itself. van Proosdij *et al.* (2016) add that AUC from presence-only data depends on prevalence. Hirzel *et al.* (2006) proposed the continuous Boyce index as a reliable measure for presence-only predictions; it is based on the ratio of predicted to expected presences across prediction classes. For Malé, presence-only measures such as the Boyce index, computed within spatial blocks, suit the evidence better than accuracy or AUC against unlabelled cells.')

H('6 DATA LIMITS ON SMALL LOW-LYING ISLANDS')
S('6.1 Elevation data')
P('Elevation is usually the most important flood predictor, but island-wide survey data for Malé are not openly available. FABDEM removes forests and buildings from the Copernicus 30 m DEM using RF models trained on reference data from 12 countries. Hawker *et al.* (2022) report that it reduces mean absolute vertical error in built-up areas from 1.61 m to 1.12 m. They also note that some building artefacts remain. Hawker *et al.* (2018) review the large vertical errors in open global DEMs and recommend stochastic ensembles of plausible DEMs rather than the false precision of a single surface. Du *et al.* (2016) found SRTM and ASTER accuracy over low coastal plains to be better than mission specifications, but related the remaining error to land cover. On an island about 1 m above sea level [Brown *et al.*, 2020], an error of about 1 m is comparable to the island\'s entire relief. In this project\'s preliminary check, FABDEM values inside Malé had a median of about 5 m and reached about 15 m. Those values are implausible for such an island and point to residual artefacts or datum differences. Global elevation should therefore enter a Malé model only as a relative rank, with its sensitivity tested.')
S('6.2 Drainage, land cover and absence data')
P('Bersabe and Jun (2025) show the value of drainage variables, but no open drainage network, inlet or pump dataset for Malé has been verified. Built-up land cover approaches saturation across the island, so land-cover layers barely distinguish one area from another. Above all, no record of observed dry locations exists, so a supervised flooded/dry classifier of the kind in Table 1 cannot be trained or validated honestly.')

H('7 RESEARCH GAPS AND PROPOSED APPROACH')
P('Four gaps emerge. (1) No peer-reviewed study maps rain-flood susceptibility in Malé or a comparable atoll city. (2) Flood susceptibility studies seldom use spatially separated validation, so their reported accuracy says little about unseen places. (3) Guidance for very small, biased presence-only flood samples in dense cities is thin and comes mostly from ecology. (4) Global elevation data are least reliable where relief is smallest.')
P('These gaps suggest a cautious design. Reports should be aggregated to a 200 m grid, keeping each record at its true scope, with unreported cells treated as unknown rather than dry. A descriptive evidence map should come first. Any model should be an exploratory ranking: a few covariates, a regularised presence-only or PU model, and a background that reflects reporting bias. It should be evaluated with spatial blocks, the Boyce index and an area-of-applicability check (Figure 2). The output is a relative susceptibility map (Figure 1 shows the study area), not a flood forecast.')
C.append(('FIG', ('fig1', 'Figure 1: Malé island proper and its four wards.')))
C.append(('FIG', ('fig2', 'Figure 2: Proposed approach and the review sections that motivate each step.')))

H('8 CONCLUSIONS')
P('Data-driven flood susceptibility mapping is well developed, but its usual recipe of many flood points, constructed non-flood samples, drainage data and random validation does not fit Malé. Methods for presence-only data, reporting bias and spatial validation, developed mainly in ecology, offer a sounder basis. The literature also shows that global elevation data are weakest on low islands. A defensible Malé study should therefore present relative susceptibility, state its uncertainty openly, and seek agency data on drainage and observed dry locations before claiming predictive skill. This review is limited by its keyword search and by the small number of urban flood studies that report spatial validation.')

